---
title: Everyday workflow
description: Edit, check, run, inspect and return to your LUI project.
sidebar:
  order: 2
---

## Edit → check → run

1. Open your project folder in VS Code.
2. Edit and save a declared Luau source.
3. Use **LUI: Check Types** and read any Problems diagnostics.
4. Use **LUI: Run App** to run the native application.
5. After editing, use **LUI: Stop App**, then run it again.

Static checking reads the declared project without running your app. A passing type check does not prove the UI works or that an application protocol is correct. Try the app's user interactions as well.

There is no native restart-on-save supervisor or application-authored `lui test` command in this SDK. Those workflows are planned.

## The layout inspector is separate

**LUI: Inspect Layout** starts another app generation to inspect its tree and resolved geometry. It does not inspect the live native window. Its state and lifetime are independent of Run App.

The inspector refuses privileged capabilities and native extensions. Use Run App for network/storage behavior. See [capabilities and execution modes](../capabilities/), then try the [complete practical projects](../recipes/) for images, time, right click and local HTTP.

## Return to a project later

Open the same project folder. Choose the exact pinned SDK if it is missing. The project records the SDK identity without committing a machine-specific SDK path. Opening a project does not automatically update its SDK or download dependencies.

## Command-line use

Use the full path to `lui.cmd`, or add the SDK's `Cli` directory to your own PATH. From the project folder, the equivalent commands are:

```powershell
lui check
lui run
lui build --output Dist/MyApp
```

For a manifest outside the current folder, pass its path explicitly. Quote paths containing spaces. Choose a new packaging output directory; existing output is preserved.

New sources must be declared in `lui.json`. See [modules and project structure](../modules/).
